```

These are technically different requests. But **don't model both immediately**. First get hall-request assignment working. Then we can decide the cleanest minimal way to handle destination selection without blowing up the design.





Yes. That is a very good separation boundary for the interview.

You can think of the first half as a **snapshot-based assignment problem**:

> A hall request arrives. At that instant, the controller looks at the current snapshot of all elevators and chooses one elevator. Once chosen, the request is handed to that elevator. What happens afterward is the elevator's own scheduling/execution problem.

So conceptually:

```text
Hall Request
   |
   v
ElevatorController
   |
   | inspect snapshot:
   | floor, direction, state, pending load
   v
SelectionStrategy
   |
   v
Chosen Elevator
```

And yes, for modeling purposes you can almost pretend **time freezes during selection**. You don't need to solve concurrency yet.

That gives you a clean interface:

```java
interface ElevatorSelectionStrategy {
    Elevator select(List<Elevator> elevators, HallRequest request);
}
```

The controller becomes very thin:

```java
class ElevatorController {
    List<Elevator> elevators;
    ElevatorSelectionStrategy strategy;

    void requestElevator(HallRequest request) {
        Elevator elevator = strategy.select(elevators, request);
        elevator.addRequest(request);
    }
}
```

The key insight is that the selection algorithm only needs to know the **observable state** of an elevator. For now, that might be:

```text
currentFloor
currentDirection
state: IDLE / MOVING
possibly number of pending stops
```

It does **not** need to know how the elevator internally stores its queues, opens doors, changes direction, etc.

So in your head, split Elevator LLD into:

```text
1. Dispatching
   "Which elevator should take this request?"

                boundary

2. Elevator scheduling/execution
   "Given requests assigned to me, what floor do I visit next?"
```

There can be correspondence between them — for example dispatch may inspect an elevator's direction or workload — but the **algorithms are separable**.

For a 30-minute round, this is exactly the kind of decomposition you want. You can finish dispatching first, then move inside one elevator and solve its request scheduling.




Yes. For your LLD round, **do not model real time**. That is unnecessary and will make the code much harder than the design problem requires.

Treat elevator movement as **logical state transitions**, not a physical simulation.

So your `Elevator` can simply have:

```java
class Elevator {
    String id;
    int currentFloor;
    Direction direction;
    ElevatorState state;

    void moveTo(int targetFloor) {
        if (targetFloor > currentFloor) {
            direction = Direction.UP;
        } else if (targetFloor < currentFloor) {
            direction = Direction.DOWN;
        }

        state = ElevatorState.MOVING;

        // Simulate movement immediately
        currentFloor = targetFloor;

        state = ElevatorState.IDLE;
        direction = Direction.NONE;
    }
}
```

That is perfectly acceptable for a 20-minute coding round.

The interviewer should understand that:

> "`moveTo()` represents commanding the physical elevator. In a production system this would be asynchronous and driven by motor/sensor events, but for this LLD implementation I'm modeling it as an immediate state transition."

That one sentence gives you the abstraction boundary.

