without introducing actual time.

The more interesting state question is actually **what happens after the elevator has multiple requests**.

Because then `moveTo()` isn't really the scheduling algorithm. It is just the actuator:

```text
Scheduler:
"What is my next floor?"

        ↓

Elevator:
"Okay, moveTo(nextFloor)"
```

So eventually I'd structure it as:

```java
void processNextRequest() {
    int nextFloor = ...;  // chosen from pending requests
    moveTo(nextFloor);
}
```

That keeps three concepts clean:

```text
Controller
    decides WHICH ELEVATOR

Elevator's scheduler
    decides WHICH FLOOR NEXT

moveTo()
    performs the logical movement
```

And yes, in a real elevator system `moveTo()` would likely send commands to hardware and state would change asynchronously based on sensor events. **For this LLD round, abstract all of that away.**




Exactly. Once an elevator can have **multiple assigned stops**, `moveTo(targetFloor)` by itself is too simplistic, because reaching one floor does **not** necessarily mean the elevator becomes `IDLE`.

So the next thing to design is the **per-elevator request scheduler**.

For your round, keep it very simple:

```text
Each Elevator owns pending destination floors.

If moving UP:
    serve higher floors in ascending order.

If moving DOWN:
    serve lower floors in descending order.

When no pending requests remain:
    state = IDLE
    direction = NONE
```

A clean structure is:

```java
class Elevator {
    String id;
    int currentFloor;
    ElevatorState state;
    Direction direction;

    TreeSet<Integer> upStops;
    TreeSet<Integer> downStops;
}
```

Why two sets?

```text
currentFloor = 5

requests = 8, 10, 3, 1

upStops   = [8, 10]
downStops = [3, 1]
```

If currently going `UP`, you keep serving:

```text
5 -> 8 -> 10
```

Then reverse:

```text
10 -> 3 -> 1
```

So now your important method is not really:

```java
moveTo(...)
```

It becomes:

```java
processNextStop()
```

Something conceptually like:

```java
void processNextStop() {

    if (direction == Direction.UP && !upStops.isEmpty()) {
        int nextFloor = upStops.pollFirst();
        moveTo(nextFloor);
        return;
    }

    if (direction == Direction.DOWN && !downStops.isEmpty()) {
        int nextFloor = downStops.pollLast();
        moveTo(nextFloor);
        return;
    }

    // Current direction exhausted.
    if (!upStops.isEmpty()) {
        direction = Direction.UP;
        state = ElevatorState.MOVING;
        moveTo(upStops.pollFirst());
    } else if (!downStops.isEmpty()) {
        direction = Direction.DOWN;
        state = ElevatorState.MOVING;
        moveTo(downStops.pollLast());
    } else {
        direction = Direction.NONE;
        state = ElevatorState.IDLE;
    }
}
```

And importantly, **`moveTo()` should not decide IDLE**.

It should only perform movement:

```java
void moveTo(int floor) {
    currentFloor = floor;
}
```

Then the scheduler decides what happens next.

That's the separation you were missing:

```text
moveTo()
    = physical/logical movement primitive

processNextStop()
    = scheduler/state machine
```

So:

```text
processNextStop()
        |
        v
pick next request
        |
        v
moveTo(nextFloor)
        |
        v
remove that request
        |
        v
any more requests?
   /             \
 yes              no
 |                 |
continue       state = IDLE
```

This is much cleaner.

And for the interview, I wouldn't initially make `moveTo()` simulate every intermediate floor either. Just:

```java
currentFloor = targetFloor;
```

because **the interesting LLD is request ordering and state transitions, not motor simulation**.

The next important question is actually: when a **new request arrives while the elevator is already moving**, which of `upStops/downStops` does it enter? That's where the elevator scheduling logic becomes interesting.


Yes — **for your interview scope, that model is fine**, and it is simpler than introducing `upStops/downStops` immediately.

What you are describing is basically:

> Each elevator maintains an **ordered queue of stops**.
> `direction` is derived from the current floor and the next queued stop.
> `move()` processes exactly one stop.

That is a very clean first implementation.

So your elevator state can be:

```java
class Elevator {
    String id;
    int currentFloor;
    Direction direction;
    ElevatorState state;

    Queue<Integer> stops;
}
```

You actually do **not need `previousFloor`** just to know direction. `currentFloor` and `nextStop` are enough.

Example:

```text
currentFloor = 0
queue = [6]
```

When `6` is added:

```text
6 > currentFloor
=> direction = UP
=> state = MOVING
```

But you don't physically move yet.

Then:

```java
move();
```

does:

```text
poll 6
currentFloor = 6
```

Now inspect the next queued stop.

Suppose:

```text
queue = [9, 4]
```

After reaching `6`:

```text
next = 9
9 > 6
=> direction stays UP
=> state stays MOVING
```

Next `move()`:

```text
6 -> 9
```

Then:

```text
next = 4
4 < 9
=> direction = DOWN
```

Next `move()`:

```text
9 -> 4
```

Then queue becomes empty:

```text
direction = NONE
state = IDLE
```

So conceptually your `move()` is:

```java
void move() {
    if (stops.isEmpty()) {
        state = ElevatorState.IDLE;
        direction = Direction.NONE;
        return;
    }

    int nextFloor = stops.poll();

    // logically reach the next stop
    currentFloor = nextFloor;

    if (stops.isEmpty()) {
        state = ElevatorState.IDLE;
        direction = Direction.NONE;
        return;
    }

    int upcomingFloor = stops.peek();

    if (upcomingFloor > currentFloor) {
        direction = Direction.UP;
    } else if (upcomingFloor < currentFloor) {
        direction = Direction.DOWN;
    } else {
        direction = Direction.NONE;
    }

    state = ElevatorState.MOVING;
}
```

And when you add the **first** stop:

```java
void addStop(int floor) {
    stops.offer(floor);

    if (state == ElevatorState.IDLE) {
        if (floor > currentFloor) {
            direction = Direction.UP;
        } else if (floor < currentFloor) {
            direction = Direction.DOWN;
        }

        state = ElevatorState.MOVING;
    }
}
```

### One important distinction

Your model answers:

> "Given the queue `[6, 9, 4]`, how does the elevator execute it?"

It does **not yet answer**:

> "Should a newly arriving floor `7` be inserted before `9`, after `9`, or at the end?"

That's the **scheduling policy**.

And that's good. Don't mix the two yet.

For your LLD:

```text
Elevator
    stores an ordered stop queue
    executes one stop at a time

Scheduler
    decides the ordering of that queue
```

So yes — **this is a better starting model for your 20-minute implementation**.

One tiny wording change: I would say the elevator starts at a **configured default floor**, maybe `0`, rather than making "all elevators always start at floor 0" a core design assumption. In code you can absolutely initialize them to `0`.



Yes — conceptually they are **two different strategies**, because they answer two different questions:

* **Elevator allocation strategy**: *Which elevator should take this new hall request?*
* **Elevator scheduling strategy**: *Given the requests already assigned to one elevator, what stop should it serve next / in what order?*

For your 30-minute round, I would usually keep them **separate conceptually**, but only extract both into interfaces if the interviewer cares about extensibility.

### Option 1: Keep them separate

Example:

```java
interface ElevatorSelectionStrategy {
    Elevator select(List<Elevator> elevators, HallRequest request);
}

interface ElevatorSchedulingStrategy {
    void addStop(Elevator elevator, int floor);
    Integer getNextStop(Elevator elevator);
}
```

Reasoning:

```text
Hall Request
    |
    v
Allocation Strategy
"Which elevator?"
    |
    v
Elevator E2
    |
    v
Scheduling Strategy
"Where does E2 go next?"
```

This follows **single responsibility** very naturally.

The allocation algorithm may care about:

```text
distance from request floor
current direction
current load
number of pending stops
```

Whereas scheduling may care about:

```text
current floor
current direction
existing stops
new destination
```

And they can change independently.

For example:

```text
Allocation:
NearestElevator
        ->
LeastLoadedElevator

Scheduling:
FIFO
        ->
SCAN / LOOK-like scheduling
```

You can change one without changing the other.

That is the strongest justification for separating them.

---

### Option 2: Put both into one strategy

You could instead have something like:

```java
interface ElevatorStrategy {
    Elevator selectElevator(...);

    void scheduleStop(Elevator elevator, int floor);
}
```

Why would someone choose this?

Because in a **small interview implementation**, allocation and scheduling can be tightly related.

Imagine a strategy like:

> Prefer an elevator already moving toward the requested floor and insert the request into its current route.

Now choosing the elevator depends heavily on **how that elevator's route is scheduled**.

For example:

```text
Request: floor 6, UP

E1:
current = 2
route = [4, 8]

E2:
current = 5
route = [1]
```

Pure "nearest elevator" might choose E2.

But a combined scheduling-aware strategy might say:

> E1 is already going upward through floor 6, so insert 6 between 4 and 8.

So allocation and scheduling aren't completely independent in sophisticated systems.

That gives a valid argument for one combined abstraction:

> "The dispatching policy owns the overall optimization of elevator movement, so it both selects an elevator and determines where the new request enters its route."

This can actually model a real dispatcher more naturally.

---

## Which should **you** choose?

For your interview:

**Separate them logically.**

But I wouldn't necessarily create two elaborate strategy frameworks immediately.

I'd do:

```java
interface ElevatorSelectionStrategy {
    Elevator select(...);
}
```

and inside `Elevator` initially keep a simple queue:

```java
Queue<Integer> stops;
```

with straightforward scheduling.

Then if the interviewer asks:

> "Can we support another scheduling algorithm?"

You say:

> "Yes. The stop-ordering logic can be extracted behind an `ElevatorSchedulingStrategy`, independent of elevator allocation."

That gives you good design **without burning coding time on abstractions you haven't needed yet**.

So think of it as:

```text
Conceptually:
2 strategies

Initial 20-minute code:
1 explicit strategy + simple scheduling logic

Extension:
extract scheduling strategy
```

The reason **not** to start with one giant `ElevatorStrategy` is that it combines two different responsibilities and becomes harder to reason about.

The reason **not** to immediately build two full strategy hierarchies is the opposite problem: **over-engineering for a 20-minute implementation**.

That middle ground is probably the strongest interview answer.





Yes, that separation is clean.

For the first pass:

```text
SelectionStrategy
= Which elevator gets the request?

Simple implementation:
choose elevator with the fewest queued stops
```

Then:

```text
SchedulingStrategy
= Where does this new stop go inside that elevator's route?
```

And you can have progressively better scheduling policies:

```text
FIFO scheduling
Just append the stop.

Direction-aware scheduling
If elevator is already moving toward that floor,
insert it into the current path.

Otherwise,
place it after the current directional run.
```

Example:

```text
Elevator at 2
Direction = UP
Queue = [5, 8]

New stop = 6

FIFO:
[5, 8, 6]

Direction-aware:
[5, 6, 8]
```

Whereas:

```text
New stop = 1

Current direction = UP

Direction-aware:
[5, 8, 1]
```

because `1` should be handled after finishing the upward run.

So your architecture becomes very intuitive:

```text
Request arrives
      |
      v
SelectionStrategy
"Which elevator?"
      |
      v
Elevator
      |
      v
SchedulingStrategy
"Where in its stops?"
      |
      v
Ordered stop queue
```

One wording adjustment: I wouldn't call the simple scheduler "random insertion." Call it **FIFO / append scheduling**. Random insertion implies nondeterministic ordering, which you probably don't want.

And this is a good interview progression: **least-loaded selection + FIFO scheduling first**, then mention direction-aware scheduling as the obvious extension.



